&gt; Violent attacks are what happens when men do not have partners, Mr. Peterson says, and society needs to work to make sure those men are married.
&gt; 
&gt; “He was angry at God because women were rejecting him,” Mr. Peterson says of the Toronto killer. “The cure for that is enforced monogamy. That’s actually why monogamy emerges.”
&gt; 
&gt; Mr. Peterson does not pause when he says this. Enforced monogamy is, to him, simply a rational solution. Otherwise women will all only go for the most high-status men, he explains, and that couldn’t make either gender happy in the end.
&gt; 
&gt; “Half the men fail,” he says, meaning that they don’t procreate. “And no one cares about the men who fail.”
&gt; 
&gt; I laugh, because it is absurd.
&gt; 
&gt; “You’re laughing about them,” he says, giving me a disappointed look. “That’s because you’re female.”

Unless the rest of Jordan Peterson's works consist of "This is how you can learn to communicate with dolphins telepathically," I feel pretty comfortable labeling him as a pretentious dickbag, actually!

His family is rich as fuck and the culture of "I vote Republican because that's what I've always done" is very strong in many parts of the state. For example, his absolutely worthless brother Greg Pence won a seat in [Indiana's 6th Congressional district despite barely having a campaign at all.](https://www.indystar.com/story/news/politics/2018/11/06/greg-pence-brother-mike-pence-wins-sixth-district-congressional-race/1896449002/). 

&gt; Greg Pence avoided talking to the media during the election and didn’t participate in any debates during his campaign, relying instead on the strong Republican slant of the district and Mike Pence’s influence with GOP heavyweights.
&gt; 
&gt; ...During his campaign, Greg Pence relied on the message that he was "ready to serve again," referencing his Marine background, rather than focusing on policy.
&gt; 
&gt; ...That same ad only provides a surface level description of his policy stances: "bring Hoosier values to Washington, make it easier for businesses to grow and create jobs and tirelessly advocate for vets."
&gt; 
&gt; There is no "issues" tab on his website. In videos on his website, however, Pence says he supports a border wall, hopes to repeal and replace the Affordable Care Act and wants to bring back infrastructure dollars.

But he was Pence's brother in a heavily red district that had always voted red, and so his campaign raised $2.5 million and off to Washington he went. It's the same district that originally elected his brother.

The political careers of Mike Pence and his brother are, ironically, examples of how a relatively small cadre of local, uninformed dipshits in an otherwise fairly bi-partisan state can have a massive national effect.

